what I love about this movie is that  there's a really big idea at the center  of this movie and I've spoken to you a  couple of times in a few different  settings and I'm fascinated to hear you  and the other people who have made this  film talk about how so many other movies  that are out lately have these kind of  dystopian views of the future and kids  killing other kids and this really is so  different from that at what point did  you and Damon or Jeff kind of sit down  and realize let's do something that is  different from all of the other  futuristic movies that we're seeing yeah  it kind of grew out of conversations  that we all had separately and together  about what happened to the future that  we used to imagine when we were kids  the future was always brighter than the  present even though the world was not  any more of a better place than it is  now I mean there were plenty of problems  when we were kids a lot of the same  problems just in manifested in different  ways but what seemed to have changed in  the years since was a feeling that you  know the future was positive and yet we  were wondering why has that gone out of  fashion why is the only credible view of  the future negative and are we kind of  hypnotizing our selves into having it be  so you know I we never set out to give  the feeling that just put on a happy  face and lay down and be happy and great  things will happen you know dream and  fabulous things will happen you know  that's not what we're saying we're  saying that the Dreaming part is step  one of you know a journey but a journey  that is absolutely within our collective  power and I think that we thought we  could make sort of a fairy tale myth  kind of thing out of that correct me if  I'm wrong this is the fourth movie that  you've directed that you've scored  because it's ratatouille The Incredibles  Mission Impossible Ghost Protocol and  this one Michael why does this  relationship work money no no  yes no that's not true you k