e started talking  and we before you knew it it was just  like we loved all of the same things we  absolutely loved all of the same things  we talked about music we talked about  everything and most of our conversation  that day I think revolved around those  kinds of things things from arch  that we loved growing up and he got to  the point where it was like Brad you  have to actually show him the movie we  brought him up to show you cuz his  flight is going to be you know and then  and I watched it and I went back and I  wrote some music and that's a whole  nother story but I think a lot of it  comes from just our love of what we do  we both love what we do so much very  basic question here is there a different  approach from where you stand if you're  composing for an animated film versus a  live-action film no and the way I like  to look at it is you know Captain Kirk a  live-action character Star Trek that guy  is no more real than that rat who wants  to cook you know there none of its real  it's all fake they're just characters  and you have to you know there are  mediums by which to work you know you  have animation you have live-action you  have puppetry you have you know cut out  paper whatever it is those are all  mediums but storytelling is storytelling  and character is character and for me  it's all about getting into the  character regardless of the medium Brad  I know that when you guys were putting  this story together and thinking about  the Frank Walker character the idea was  to have a George Clooney type so you're  very fortunate to get him was he willing  to be bounced around and flown up to  space so soon after doing gravity you  know he read the script so I assumed  that he was ready for it yeah he he  basically said when Damon and I were  coming up the driveway he met us at his  house in LA and basically said very  nicely I don't really do these kind of  movies but if you want to pitch it what  the heck you know and we were shocked  when he was like intrigued and wanted to  go in but I think it was because of the  you know the underlying themes of the  film and the weird relationships in the  film and the fact that it's kind of a  really unusual film in usual film  clothing and  so I think that that's what drew him in  he's joked a couple times that I guess  the description of Frank Walker's you  know crusty old man and he was thought  hey wait a minute come on what are you  trying to say is that just a line that  he's said for the press tour was that  actually something he was concerned  about it didn't say crusty old man  crusty old bitter guy no it was it was I  mean the movie always started out with  him as a child and it was more somebody  who's disappointed in the future and the  future is sort of represented by the  Athena character it's really you know a  weird movie in that there's no typical  romance in it the romance is all between  the characters and the future it's not  between them as much and so I think he  was just intrigued by those ideas my  understanding is that a several of your  lead actors Britt Robertson particularly  in her first round of auditions she was  only given a handful of pages of the  script which I know was difficult for  her I obviously understand why you did  that you wanted to keep the secrecy was  it hard then to pull the trigger on some  of the big casting decisions given the  fact that these were people that hadn't  read the whole thing and weren't able to  really sink their teeth into the whole  script well we they could read it when  it was close to the final thing the  problem is you can't just go make a  movie now it especially if the budget  gets above a certain level it's like  people want to tell everyone what the  movie is and it's not like we're trying  to like we have this epic secret that  we're trying to keep it will just blow  your minds when we finally reveal it we  just don't want the story to be told  before we tell it yeah that's that's  it's as 